        <div class="container">
          <div>
          <div class="breadcrumbs">
            <a href="/">Home</a>
            <span>&#8250;</span>
            <a href="/seo">SEO</a>
            <span>&#8250;</span>
            <a href="/seo/backlinks">Link Building</a>
            <span>&#8250;</span>
            <span>Travel Posting Sites</span>
          </div>

          <span class="card-icon pillar-icon"><i class="fa-solid fa-plane-departure" aria-hidden="true"></i></span>
          <p class="eyebrow">Link building</p>
          <h1>{title}</h1>
          <p class="pillar-intro">{intro}</p>
          <PageByline />
          </div>
          <div class="posting-hero-figure">
            <img src="/images/posting/travel-posting-sites.png" width="1200" height="675" alt={title} loading="eager" decoding="async" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-body">
        <div class="container article-shell">
          <div class="prose pillar-body">
            <p class="prose-lede">Travel publishing splits into four tiers, and where you can land a byline depends on whether you have clips, a niche or just a trip report.</p>

            <p>The national travel media do not take guest posts. The points-and-miles outlets take data-led analysis. The independent blogs are open. The booking platforms have editorial sections. The list of 100 is below, grouped by tier.</p>

            <h2 id="tier">Pick your tier first</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Examples</th><th>How you get in</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>National travel media</td><td>Nat Geo Travel, Travel + Leisure, Conde Nast Traveler, Lonely Planet, AFAR</td><td>Not guest posts. A pitch from a working travel journalist with clips, or exclusive data</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Points, miles &amp; deals</td><td>The Points Guy, NerdWallet Travel, Going, Skyscanner, The Flight Deal</td><td>Data-led analysis with real numbers; award routing, valuations, fare trends</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Independent travel blogs</td><td>Nomadic Matt, The Broke Backpacker, Earth Trekkers, Against the Compass, Wander-Lush</td><td>First-hand guides with a specific angle and your own photos</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Booking &amp; planning platforms</td><td>Hostelworld, World Nomads, Rough Guides, Worldpackers, TravelLadies</td><td>Practical destination content and traveller-safety pieces</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="metrics">What the metrics mean</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it is</th><th>How much it matters</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>Domain Rating (DR)</td><td>Ahrefs' 0 to 100 score for a whole site's backlink profile</td><td>A useful filter, not a verdict</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Domain Authority (DA)</td><td>Moz's equivalent 0 to 100 score</td><td>Same idea as DR</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Page Authority (PA)</td><td>Moz's score for one specific page</td><td>Matters once you know which page your post will live on</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>URL Rating (UR)</td><td>Ahrefs' score for one specific URL</td><td>Same purpose as PA</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Monthly Traffic</td><td>Estimated organic visits per month</td><td>The most honest signal that a link will be seen</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Here is the deal:</strong> travel is one of the most saturated content niches, and Google's updates have hammered thin destination content. A placement on a site with a real audience and first-hand reporting is worth far more than a link on a blog that has not posted in two years.</p></div>

            <h2 id="list">The 100 travel sites</h2>
            <p>Domain Rating, Domain Authority, Page Authority, URL Rating and estimated monthly traffic. Every domain was checked live in September 2026; closed and dormant blogs were replaced with live equivalents. Metrics are third-party estimates and shift over time.</p>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>#</th><th>Website</th><th>DR</th><th>DA</th><th>PA</th><th>UR</th><th>Monthly traffic</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {SITES.map((s) => (
                    <tr>
                      <td>{s.n}</td>
                      <td>{s.site}</td>
                      <td>{s.dr}</td>
                      <td>{s.da}</td>
                      <td>{s.pa}</td>
                      <td>{s.ur}</td>
                      <td>{s.traffic}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="pitch">The pitch that works for travel editors</h2>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Lead with the angle, not the destination.</strong> Not "48 hours in Lisbon." Something like "how Lisbon's new tourist tax actually changes a budget trip."</li>
              <li><strong>Show clips and photos.</strong> Editors will not take a destination piece from someone whose work and images they cannot see.</li>
              <li><strong>Pitch the section.</strong> "For your points-and-miles coverage" or "for the solo-travel series" shows you read the site.</li>
              <li><strong>Bring first-hand detail.</strong> Prices you paid, transport you took, a mistake you made and what it cost.</li>
              <li><strong>Match the format.</strong> National media want a reported feature. Points sites want a data piece. Blogs want a practical guide.</li>
              <li><strong>Disclose any tourism board, hotel or tour sponsorship</strong> behind the trip.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>The test:</strong> would an editor run this if you sold no tours or courses? If yes, pitch it. If it is an ad for your itinerary product, it goes in the bin.</p></div>

            <h2 id="mistakes">What not to do</h2>
            <ul class="check-list">
              <li>Pitch a generic "top 10 things to do in Bali" listicle. Every site has one.</li>
              <li>Write a destination piece about a place you have not been.</li>
              <li>Send the same pitch to a national title and a backpacker blog.</li>
              <li>Stuff affiliate links, or point exact-match anchors ("best travel insurance") at your product.</li>
              <li>Use AI-generated destination content with no first-hand detail. Google and editors both catch it.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> a few placements on active sites with real audiences, built on first-hand travel, will do more for rankings and bookings than a long list of dormant travel-blog links. Use this list to find your few.</p></div>
          </div>

          <aside class="article-toc">
            <h4>On this page</h4>
            <ul>
              {toc.map((item) => (
                <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.text}</a></li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div id="faq">
